# MEMORY INDEX — {{TARGET}}

This is the canonical index for durable harness memory. Do not scan every memory file; select only entries needed for the current task.

## Index

| ID | Path | Summary | Read when | Source | Last verified | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| - | - | No durable memory registered | - | - | {{CREATED_DATE}} | empty |

Status is one of `active`, `superseded`, `archived`, or `empty`. Every `active` path must exist, and one path must not appear under multiple IDs.

## Update contract

1. Before writing, search this index and the target path for duplicates, conflicts, and prior sources.
2. Create, move, rename, supersede, or archive memory in the same transaction as its index-row update. Keep each document within the spec line budget.
3. Update paths and references together. When history matters, keep the prior entry as `superseded` and link its successor in the summary.
4. Prefer `archived` or `superseded` to deletion. Do not overwrite, delete, or rename user-owned or unknown-source memory without explicit approval.
5. At session end and after harness maintenance, verify active paths, duplicate IDs or paths, orphaned documents, and last-verified dates.
6. The index is discovery data. `harness-spec.json` and `HARNESS.md` are canonical for preservation policy and read routing; record canonical changes in `ledger/DECISIONS.md` and `ledger/journal.jsonl`.

## Inclusion rules

- Index durable memory only. Current progress belongs in `state/state.json`; execution events belong in append-only `ledger/journal.jsonl`.
- Store a concise summary and source path instead of a raw dump. Never copy secrets, credentials, or personal data into memory.
- Existing project documents may remain in place. Index their original paths and identify external ownership in the source field.
